KNOWNBUG
block_declaration_assignment1.sv
--bound 2
^\[main\.p0\] always \(##1 main\.y == 5\): PROVED up to bound 2$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
A static variable declared inside a procedural (always) block with an
initializer, e.g. `reg [3:0] tmp = 4'd5;`, loses its initial value. The
property `##1 y == 5` is REFUTED although it must be PROVED: per
IEEE 1800-2017 6.21 and 10.5 a static variable declaration assignment is
performed once, before any initial/always procedure starts, just like a
module-level variable with an initializer.

Root cause: in src/verilog/verilog_rtl.cpp, verilog_rtl_buildert::build_statement,
the ID_decl case only calls record_variable() for each declarator and never
records declarator.value() into rtl.initial_values. Contrast
build_module_item_decl, which writes the declarator value into
rtl.initial_values for module-level variables. --show-rtl shows
`main.y[3:0] register, next-state value: main.0.tmp` with no initial value
for main.0.tmp.
